# Findings gitleaks reported, reviewed, and deliberately silenced — by
# FINGERPRINT, which pins each one to a single line of a single file in a single
# commit. Nothing here can hide a finding in any other commit, including a future
# one, because the commit hash is part of the match.
#
# That narrowness is the whole point and it is enforced rather than promised:
# `ci/gitleaks-ignores-are-pinned-to-one-commit` in tests/unit/repo-config.test.ts
# rejects any entry that is not a 40-character commit hash followed by a path, a
# rule and a line. A path-wide or rule-wide allowlist — which is what this file
# would become if someone answered a future finding with `tests/**` — fails that
# guard.
#
# ---
#
# CFV1-RUN. Three test credentials were committed as literals in 2384f6b, and
# gitleaks matched them as `generic-api-key` at entropy 4.39. It was RIGHT to: a
# credential-shaped literal in a public repository is one a scanner cannot tell
# from a real secret, which is why the SL5 spike mints its throwaway credential
# instead of committing one.
#
# They were removed in bc6c323 — the test credentials are now assembled from an
# obviously non-secret phrase, so nothing in the tree carries the shape any more.
# What remains is the history: gitleaks scans the pull request's commit range,
# and rewriting that history is denied in this environment and is not something
# to work around on a shared branch. So the three historical findings are pinned
# here, and only those three.
#
# None of the three is or was a live credential: each was a fixture string in a
# test file, and the instances they configured live for the length of a test.
